# Copy to .estate and fill in. Gitignored, because the real one names the client.
#
# One line per shop:
#   shop|remote dir|database here|admin port|storefront port|storefront domain|rails domain|live shop url|order prefix
#
# Read by script/shop_host as, in this order:
#   S_NAME S_DIR S_DB S_PORT S_SPORT S_DOMAIN S_STORE S_LIVE S_PREFIX
#
# ── which of the two domains is which ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Field 6 is the customer facing storefront, on the storefront port.
# Field 7 is Rails: /admin and the Store API, on the admin port.
#
# That is the mapping the script actually writes into nginx:
#
#   write_vhost "$S_STORE"  "$S_PORT"   "Rails: admin and the Store API"
#   write_vhost "$S_DOMAIN" "$S_SPORT"  "the customer facing storefront"
#
# This header used to name them the other way round, and it is worth saying why
# the wrong order is easy to believe: field 6 holds a name like new<shop>, which
# reads as a staging admin, and field 7 holds store.<shop>, which reads as the
# shop. It is the reverse. The convention was corrected by the client after the
# first pass had it backwards, and cmd_web in the same script still carries the
# old mapping, so the header is the only place someone would look to settle it.
#
# Getting it wrong does not fail loudly. It issues a certificate for the right
# hostname and points it at the wrong port, so the storefront answers on the
# admin address and /admin answers on the shop address.
#
# Use - for a domain that does not exist yet; the script then refuses to publish
# that shop rather than inventing a hostname.
#
# example|example_com|example_migrate|3011|3021|newexample.example.com|store.example.com|https://example.com|EXP
#
# And one LIVE_DOMAINS line: every hostname that serves real customers. The
# script checks every domain against this before touching nginx or certbot, so a
# typo cannot put a review copy in front of real shoppers. Space separated.
#
# LIVE_DOMAINS=example.com www.example.com shop.example.com
